{"id":28,"role":"Assistant","content":"Let me search the literature for the Osterwalder-Seiler transfer matrix construction.","timestamp":1784083795,"token_count":25,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"query\": \"Osterwalder Seiler transfer matrix lattice gauge theory reflection positivity\", \"source\": \"arxiv\"}","name":"search_literature"},"id":"call_00_RuYOA7V3sHhY6Ajd2qPR7122","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\": \"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean\", \"offset\": 1, \"limit\": 50}","name":"read_file"},"id":"call_01_PVNBhfIFBe0XMjjkPgkm7958","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":8447,"reasoning_content":"Now I need to understand the reflection of `G`. Let me also look at the lemmas about the reflection of the action terms.\n\nLooking at the reflection:\n- `θU` at site n, direction μ is: if μ=0 then U(θ n, μ)⁻¹ else U(θ n, μ)\n\nSo `reflectSitePeriodic` maps time t ↦ -t in ZMod T. This means:\n- If n has positive signed time, θ n has negative signed time.\n- If n has negative signed time, θ n has positive signed time.\n- If n has interface (signed time = 0), θ n also has interface (since -0 = 0).\n\nSo reflection swaps positive and negative sites, and leaves interface sites invariant.\n\nNow, for the reflection of the action:\n- `wilsonActionOSPositive(N T L β (θU)) = wilsonActionOSNegative(N T L β U)` — this swaps positive and negative actions.\n- `wilsonActionOSInterface(N T L β (θU)) = wilsonActionOSInterface(N T L β U)` — interface is invariant.\n\nThese are already proved.\n\nNow, back to `transferMatrix_identity`. Let me think about what the identity means mathematically.\n\nThe LHS is:\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) where μ₀ is the product Haar measure on SU(N)^{all links}.\n\nUsing the measure factorization μ₀ ≅ μ⁺ × μ⁻ × μ⁰ (a product measure), and using the decomposition of U into (U⁺, U⁻, U⁰), we can write:\n\nLHS = ∫_{μ⁺}∫_{μ⁻}∫_{μ⁰} G(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)·G(θ(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁰ dμ⁻ dμ⁺\n\nNow, G(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2)\n\nSince S_OS⁺(U) depends only on U⁺ and S_OS_int(U) depends only on U⁰ (and possibly U⁺ and U⁻), let me think about how these actions decompose.\n\nActually wait - the action terms are defined in terms of the link variables, not just the sites. A plaquette at (n, μ, ν) involves links at sites n, addVector(n, μ), addVector(n, μ, ν), addVector(n, ν). The positive plaquettes are those where all four corners have positive signed time. But the links involved in such a plaquette can be at positive-time sites (because the links go from one positive-time corner to another positive-time corner, and the intermediate sites are also at positive time).\n\nWait, is this true? Let me check: if n has positive time, and addVector(n, μ) has positive time, is it guaranteed that the link between them (at site n in direction μ) is a positive link? \n\nThe definition of `positiveSites` is `signedTime T n.time > 0`. So a positive link is at a positive site. The link from n to addVector(n, μ) is stored at site n with direction μ. So if n has positive time, the link is positive regardless of where the target site is.\n\nBut for `S_OS⁺`, the condition is that ALL FOUR corners have positive signed time. This ensures n is positive, addVector(n, μ) is positive, etc. So all four links involved (at n, addVector(n, μ), addVector(n, μ, ν), addVector(n, ν)) have starting points at positive time, hence are positive links.\n\nWait, that's not quite right. Let me trace through a plaquette:\n\n```\nn ──(n, μ)──→ n+e_μ\n│              │\n(n, ν)      (n+e_μ, ν)\n↓              ↓\nn+e_ν ─(n+e_ν, μ)─→ n+e_μ+e_ν\n```\n\nThe four links are:\n1. U(n, μ) — stored at n\n2. U(n+e_μ, ν) — stored at n+e_μ  \n3. U(n+e_μ+e_ν, μ)⁻¹ — this is the reverse of U(n+e_μ+e_ν, μ), stored at n+e_μ+e_ν\n4. U(n+e_ν, ν)⁻¹ — stored at n+e_ν\n\nSo the links involved are at sites: n, n+e_μ, n+e_μ+e_ν, n+e_ν.\n\nIf all four corners (n, n+e_μ, n+e_μ+e_ν, n+e_ν) have positive signed time, then all four link-sites