w, LHS = ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U)\n\nLet me write LHS more explicitly. Since U = merge(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰):\n\nLHS = ∫ f(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)·f(θU⁺,θU⁻,θU⁰)·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(U⁺)+S_OS⁻(U⁻)+S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰))) dμ⁺(U⁺)dμ⁻(U⁻)dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nRHS = ∫ f(U⁺,𝟙,U⁰)²·exp(-β·(2·S_OS⁺(U⁺)+S_OS_int(U⁺,𝟙,U⁰)))·(∫ exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)) dμ⁺(U⁺)dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nThese are clearly different. So the identity as stated is false.\n\nLet me think about what the correct OS transfer matrix identity should be.\n\nHmm, maybe I'm misunderstanding the definition of G_plus. Let me re-read:\n\n```lean4\nnoncomputable def G_plus (N T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T) (β : ℝ)\n    (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ) (cfg : PosInterfaceConfig N T L) : ℝ :=\n  G N T L hT β f (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (positiveSites T L ∪ interfaceSites T L) cfg)\n```\n\nSo G_plus extends cfg (which lives on positive ∪ interface sites) to a full link variable by setting NEGATIVE sites to 1, and then applies G.\n\nBut in the OS formalism, the transfer matrix acts on functions on the interface configuration space. Let me think about what the correct G_plus should be.\n\nActually, in the OS paper, the correct decomposition is:\n\nThe function F depends on variables in the positive time region (including the interface at time 0). The reflection θF depends on variables in the negative time region. The inner product:\n\n<F, TF> = ∫ F(U⁺, U⁰)·(TF)(U⁺, U⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nwhere T acts as:\n\n(TF)(U⁺, U⁰) = ∫ F(U⁻, U⁰)·exp(-S_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nThe key identity is:\n\n∫ F(U⁺, U⁰)·F(θU⁻, θU⁰)·exp(-S_W(U)) dμ₀ = ∫ F(U⁺, U⁰)·(TF)(U⁺, U⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nWait, but here F in the LHS depends only on (U⁺, U⁰), while in the RHS, F also depends only on (U⁺, U⁰). The difference is that in the LHS, we also have the full measure μ₀ and the Boltzmann weight exp(-S_W), while in the RHS, we have the product measure μ⁺ × μ⁰ and the transfer matrix T acting.\n\nLet me write this more precisely:\n\n∫ F(U⁺, U⁰)·F(θU⁻,