{"id":32,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\periodic_bc_plan.md\n-- 141 lines, 5408 bytes --\n  1 | # Implementation Plan: Periodic Boundary Conditions for Reflection Positivity\n  2 | \n  3 | ## Objective\n  4 | \n  5 | Replace the current `Z4Site = ℤ⁴` lattice with a lattice that has periodic boundary\n  6 | conditions in the time direction, making the `hadd` hypothesis satisfiable for finite\n  7 | lattices. This enables a genuine proof of the Osterwalder-Seiler reflection positivity.\n  8 | \n  9 | ## Design\n 10 | \n 11 | ### Step 1: Define `TimeIndex` as a finite cyclic group\n 12 | \n 13 | Add to `Lattice.lean`:\n 14 | \n 15 | ```lean\n 16 | /-- Time index for a finite lattice with periodic boundary conditions.\n 17 |     ℤ_T = ZMod T where T : ℕ is the number of time slices. -/\n 18 | abbrev TimeIndex (T : ℕ) : Type := ZMod T\n 19 | ```\n 20 | \n 21 | Or more generally, keep `Z4Site` as `ℤ⁴` but define `addVector` and `reflectSite`\n 22 | to work modulo `T` for the time coordinate when the lattice is a product of intervals.\n 23 | \n 24 | **Recommendation**: Define a structure `PeriodicLattice (T L : ℕ)` that represents\n 25 | the product `(ZMod T) × (ZMod L)³` and provides `addVector` and `reflectSite` with\n 26 | modular arithmetic.\n 27 | \n 28 | ### Step 2: Modify `addVector` for periodic BCs\n 29 | \n 30 | ```lean\n 31 | def addVectorPeriodic (T : ℕ) (n : ZMod T × ZMod T × ZMod T × ZMod T) (μ : Fin 4) : ... :=\n 32 |   match μ with\n 33 |   | 0 => (n.1 + 1, n.2.1, n.2.2.1, n.2.2.2)  -- wraps in ZMod\n 34 |   | 1 => (n.1, n.2.1 + 1, n.2.2.1, n.2.2.2)\n 35 |   | 2 => (n.1, n.2.1, n.2.2.1 + 1, n.2.2.2)\n 36 |   | 3 => (n.1, n.2.1, n.2.2.1, n.2.2.2 + 1)\n 37 | ```\n 38 | \n 39 | The `ZMod` addition automatically wraps around modulo T/L.\n 40 | \n 41 | ### Step 3: Modify `reflectSite` for periodic BCs\n 42 | \n 43 | ```lean\n 44 | def reflectSitePeriodic (T : ℕ) (n : ZMod T × ZMod T × ZMod T × ZMod T) :=\n 45 |   (-n.1, n.2.1, n.2.2.1, n.2.2.2)\n 46 | ```\n 47 | \n 48 | In `ZMod T`, `-k` is `T-k` (mod T), so `-(-k) = k`. This is involutive.\n 49 | \n 50 | ### Step 4: Update `Z4Site` or create a parallel type\n 51 | \n 52 | Decision: Either:\n 53 | - **A1**: Replace `Z4Site` everywhere. This is invasive.\n 54 | - **A2**: Create `PeriodicZ4Site T L` as a separate type and dual-wire the\n 55 |   definitions. This adds complexity but preserves backward compatibility.\n 56 | - **A3**: Parameterize `Z4Site` by the time lattice type.\n 57 | \n 58 | **Recommendation**: A1 is cleanest now that the project is still in early stages.\n 59 | Replace `Z4Site : Type := ℤ × ℤ × ℤ × ℤ` with `PeriodicSite (T L : ℕ) : Type :=\n 60 | ZMod T × ZMod L × ZMod L × ZMod L`.\n 61 | \n 62 | ### Step 5: Re-prove the basic lemmas\n 63 | \n 64 | - `reflectSite_involution`: `reflectSite (reflectSite n) = n` with ZMod arithmetic.\n 65 | - `reflection_involution_z4`: `reflectLinkVariableZ4 N (reflectLinkVariableZ4 N U) = U`.\n 66 | \n 67 | ### Step 6: Prove action invariance under reflection\n 68 | \n 69 | ```lean\n 70 | lemma wilsonActionFinite_reflection_invariant (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) \n 71 |     (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) :\n 72 |     wilsonActionFinite N β (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) (reflectLinkVariableZ4 N U) =\n 73 |     wilsonActionFinite N β (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) U := ...\n 74 | ```\n 75 | \n 76 | Proof: Each plaquette contribution at (n, μ, ν) maps to a contribution at (reflectSite n, μ', ν')\n 77 | under reflection. The sum over all sites covers each plaquette exactly once because\n 78 | reflection is a bijection on the finite lattice.\n 79 | \n 80 | ### Step 7: Action decomposition (Osterwalder-Seiler)\n 81 | \n 82 | Define the positive-time, negative-time, and interface parts of the action:\n 83 | \n 84 | ```lean\n 85 | def wilsonActionPositive (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) : ℝ :=\n 86 |   ∑ n ∈ sites_with_time_gt_0, ∑ μ ν, plaquetteContribution N β U n μ ν\n 87 | \n 88 | def wilsonActionNegative (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) : ℝ :=\n 89 |   ∑ n ∈ sites_with_time_lt_0, ∑ μ ν, plaquetteContribution N β U n μ ν\n 90 | \n 91 | def wilsonActionInterface (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) : ℝ :=\n 92 |   ∑ n ∈ sites_with_time_eq_0, ∑ μ ν, plaquetteContribution N β U n μ ν\n 93 | ```\n 94 | \n 95 | Prove:\n 96 | 1. `total_decomposition`: `S_W = S⁺ + S⁻ + S_int`\n 97 | 2. `neg_reflection`: `S⁻[U] = S⁺[θU]`\n 98 | 3. `interface_symmetric`: `S_int[θU] = S_int[U]`\n 99 | \n100 | ### Step 8: Complete the reflection positivity proof\n101 | \n102 | Using the decomposition:\n103 | \n104 | ⟨f·θf⟩ = (1/Z) ∫ f(U) f(θU) exp(-β(S⁺+S⁻+S_int)) dU\n105 |        = (1/Z) ∫ f(U) exp(-βS⁺) · f(θU) exp(-βS⁺[θU]) · exp(-βS_int) dU\n106 |        = (1/Z) ∫ |f(U) exp(-βS⁺[U]) · sqrt(exp(-βS_int[U]))|² dU  [with change of variables]\n107 |        ≥ 0\n108 | \n109 | The key change of variables: split the integration variables into U⁺ (positive time) and\n110 | U⁻ (negative time), and replace U⁻ by θU⁺.\n111 | \n112 | ### Step 9: Verify the Z4Expectation structure\n113 | \n114 | Update `wilsonZ4Expectation` to work with the new lattice type. The `hadd` hypothesis\n115 | is no longer needed (or is trivially satisfied by the periodic structure).\n116 | \n117 | ## Dependencies between steps\n118 | \n119 | ```\n120 | Step 1 (type definition)\n121 |   → Step 2, 3 (basic operations)\n122 |     → Step 4 (integration into existing code)\n123 |       → Step 5 (basic lemmas)\n124 |         → Step 6 (action invariance)\n125 |           → Step 7 (decomposition)\n126 |             → Step 8 (final proof)\n127 |               → Step 9 (structure update)\n128 | ```\n129 | \n130 | ## Estimated effort\n131 | \n132 | - Steps 1-4: 1-2 sessions (type refactoring, updating dependent definitions)\n133 | - Step 5: 1 session (proving basic lemmas in the new setting)\n134 | - Steps 6-7: 2-3 sessions (combinatorial decomposition of the action)\n135 | - Steps 8-9: 1-2 sessions (final reflection positivity proof, structure cleanup)\n136 | \n137 | Total: 5-8 sessions of focused work.\n138 | \n139 | ## Test: Build after each step\n140 | \n141 | After each step, run `lake build` to ensure the project still compiles.\n","timestamp":1784083813,"token_count":1924,"tool_call_id":"call_00_7gI5ToeFvEcRqirEfk6Y3287","full_token_estimate":1419,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"docs/periodic_bc_plan.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":141,"byte_count":5408,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":9,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":33,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1784083840,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\": \"def signedTime\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"call_00_tXvC5tGhUVT7FlikB3YD4617","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\": \"def periodicTime|signedTime|PositiveTime|InterfaceTime|NegativeTime\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"call_01_B3qf2EbJR2vmhWNAUsVL9671","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":2029,"reasoning_content":"Looking at Step 8 in the plan:\n\n```\n⟨f·θf⟩ = (1/Z) ∫ f(U) f(θU) exp(-β(S⁺+S⁻+S_int)) dU\n       = (1/Z) ∫ f(U) exp(-βS⁺) · f(θU) exp(-βS⁺[θU]) · exp(-βS_int) dU\n       = (1/Z) ∫ |f(U) exp(-βS⁺[U]) · sqrt(exp(-βS_int[U]))|² dU  [with change of variables]\n       ≥ 0\n```\n\nThis is a different approach from the transfer matrix! The plan says that reflection positivity follows from a simple change of variables (replacing U⁻ by θU⁺), which expresses the integrand as a perfect square.\n\nWait, but this is too simple — it doesn't account for the fact that f can depend on negative links too. The change of variables replaces U⁻ with θU⁺, which means we need to consider f(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) → f(U⁺, θU⁺, U⁰). This only works if f doesn't depend on U⁻ separately from U⁺.\n\nActually, I think the plan in Step 8 is a bit hand-wavy. Let me look at the actual approach in the code.\n\nThe `transferMatrix_identity` approach is the more rigorous OS approach. But as I've identified, there's a fundamental issue.\n\nLet me look at what Osterwalder-Seiler actually proves. The standard result is:\n\nFor a function F that depends only on gauge fields in the positive time region (t > 0), including the interface at t = 0, we have:\n\n⟨θF·F⟩ ≥ 0\n\nwhere θF is the reflection of F. The proof uses the transfer matrix T and shows that:\n\n⟨θF·F⟩ = ⟨F, T·F⟩ ≥ 0\n\nbecause T is a positive operator.\n\nIn our case, the observable is f(U)·f(θU), which is of the form F·θF where F = f. Wait, no — if we define F(U) = f(U), then θF(U) = f(θU), and the observable is F(U)·θF(U) = f(U)·f(θU). So yes, it IS of the form F·θF!\n\nBut the OS theorem requires F to depend only on positive-time fields. If f depends on all links, then F = f depends on all links, which violates this assumption.\n\nHowever, we're dividing the Wilson action into S_W = S⁺ + S⁻ + S_int, and then defining:\n\nG(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_int(U)/2)\n\nso that G(U)·G(θU) = f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U))\n\nNow, G(U) still depends on all links through f. So the standard OS argument still doesn't apply.\n\nUnless... the function G(U) can be written as:\n\nG(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) = G⁺(U⁺, U⁰)·G⁻(U⁻, U⁰)\n\nfor some G⁺ depending only on positive+interface links and G⁻ depending only on negative+interface links. If that were the case, then:\n\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ G⁺(U⁺, U⁰)·G⁻(U⁻, U⁰)·G⁺(θU⁻, θU⁰)·G⁻(θU⁺, θU⁰) dμ₀\n\nwhich would separate into positive and negative parts. But this factorization is not true for general G.\n\nHmm, I think I need to reconsider the whole approach. Let me look at how the code currently handles this.\n\nActually, looking at the code more carefully, I notice that the `transferMatrix_identity` is used to prove `integral_G_thetaG_nonneg`, which is then supposed to fill in the last sorry in `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive`.\n\nBut the comment in `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` says:\n\n```\n-- UPDATE: The lemma `integral_G_thetaG_nonneg` in `TransferMatrix.lean`\n-- proves exactly this statement using the transfer matrix framework.\n-- It depends on `transferMatrix_identity` (which remains a `sorry` in\n-- TransferMatrix.lean). The `transferMatrix_positive` lemma is now proved\n```\n\nSo the plan is:\n1. `transferMatrix_positive` is proved (done ✓)\n2. `transferMatrix_identity` needs to be proved\n3. Then `integral_G_thetaG_nonneg` follows\n4. Then the sorry in `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` is replaced\n\nBut as I've identified, `transferMatrix_identity` as stated is likely incorrect for general f. So we need to either:\n1. Fix the statement of `transferMatrix_identity` to be correct\n2. Or find a different approach to prove `integral_G_thetaG_nonneg`\n3. Or prove `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` directly without the transfer matrix\n\nLet me think about approach 3: Can we prove `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` directly?\n\nThe statement is:\n0 ≤ ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U) / Z\n\nSince Z > 0, we just need to show the numerator is ≥ 0.\n\nThe numerator is: ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)\n\nNow, notice that S_W(U) = S_W(θU) (the action is reflection-invariant). This is already proved as a lemma.\n\nSo we can use the change of variables U → θU:\n∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U) = ∫ f(θU)·f(U)·exp(-β·S_W(θU)) dμ₀(θU) = ∫ f(θU)·f(U)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U) (if the measure is invariant)\n\nThis is just the same expression. So that doesn't help.\n\nBut here's an idea: Can we write the integral as a sum of squares?\n\nIf we decompose the lattice into time slices, we can view the integral as:\n\n∫ exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰))·exp(-β·S_OS⁻(U⁻))·f(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)·f(θU⁺,θU⁻,θU⁰) dμ₀\n\nNow θ swaps positive and negative links. If we make the change of variables:\nV⁺ = U⁺, V⁰ = U⁰, V⁻ = θU⁺\n\nThen the integrand becomes something like... but this doesn't work because f depends on U⁻ independently.\n\nOK, let me think about this differently. Perhaps the correct approach for general f is not through the transfer matrix at all, but through the spectral representation.\n\nIn the OS reconstruction theorem, the proof of reflection positivity for general F (supported on positive time) uses the transfer matrix, which is defined by the conditional expectation. But if F depends on all links, the argument breaks down.\n\nWait, I think I'm confused about the structure of the proof. Let me re-read the reflection positivity condition:\n\n```lean4\nlemma gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\n    (hT : Odd T) (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ) :\n    0 ≤ gibbsExpectation N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n      (λ U => f U * reflectObservable N f U) := ...\n```\n\nHere the expectation is:\n⟨f·θf⟩ = (1/Z) ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)\n\nThe integrand is f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)).\n\nNow, let H(U) = √(exp(-β·S_W(U))) = exp(-β·S_W(U)/2).\n\nThen:\nf(U)·exp(-β·S_W(U)/2) =: F(U)\n\nAnd the integrand becomes F(U)·F(θU). \n\nNow, if F depended only on positive-time links, then the transfer matrix argument would give reflection positivity directly. But F = f·exp(-β·S_W/2) depends on all links.\n\nWait, but what if we use the factorization of S_W into S_OS⁺, S_OS⁻, S_OS_int?\n\nG(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2)\nG(θU) = f(θU)·exp(-β·S_OS⁻(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2)\n\nSo f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) = G(U)·G(θU).\n\nNow, G(U) still depends on all links through f. But the beauty of the OS construction is that the transfer matrix argument works for ANY function G that depends on all links, because we can always write:\n\n∫ G(U)·G(θU)·exp(-β·S_int(U)) dμ₀(U) = ∫ G_plus(u)·T(G_plus)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nwhere G_plus(u) = G(U⁺, 𝟙, U⁰) and T includes the integral over U⁻.\n\nWait, let me check if this actually works mathematically. Let me define:\n\nG_plus(U⁺, U⁰) = G(U⁺, 𝟙, U⁰)\n\nThen:\n∫ G(U)·G(θU)·exp(-β·S_int(U)) dμ₀(U)\n\n= ∫ G(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)·G(θU⁺, θU⁻, θU⁰)·exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nHmm, but the transfer matrix is defined as:\n(T ψ)(U⁺, U⁰) = ∫ ψ(U⁻, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nSo the RHS of the desired identity is:\n∫ G_plus(U⁺, U⁰)·(T G_plus)(U⁺, U⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n= ∫ G_plus(U⁺, U⁰)·(∫ G_plus(U⁻, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n= ∫ G(U⁺, 𝟙, U⁰)·G(U⁻, 𝟙, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nWait, but this is different from the LHS! The LHS has:\nG(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)·G(θU⁺, θU⁻, θU⁰) = G(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)·G(U⁻, U⁺, U⁰)\n\nA