{"id":37,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\ReflectionPositivity.lean\n-- 1526 lines, 94678 bytes --\n150 | /--\n151 | Signed lift of `ZMod T` to `ℤ`. For an odd T, this gives a symmetric representation\n152 | where `t` and `-t` map to opposite integers. The element `0` maps to `0`.\n153 | -/\n154 | noncomputable def signedTime (T : ℕ) (t : ZMod T) : ℤ :=\n155 |   if h : (t.val : ℕ) ≤ (T-1)/2 then (t.val : ℤ) else (t.val : ℤ) - (T : ℤ)\n156 | \n157 | lemma signedTime_neg (T : ℕ) [NeZero T] (t : ZMod T) (hT : Odd T) : signedTime T (-t) = -signedTime T t := by\n158 |   unfold signedTime\n159 |   have h_val_lt : t.val < T := ZMod.val_lt t\n160 |   rcases hT with ⟨k, hT⟩\n161 |   have hTodd : (T : ℤ) = 2*(k : ℤ) + 1 := by exact_mod_cast hT\n162 |   by_cases hzero : t = 0\n163 |   · subst hzero; simp\n164 |   · have hzero_val : t.val ≠ 0 := by rwa [ZMod.val_ne_zero]\n165 |     haveI : NeZero t := ⟨hzero⟩\n166 |     have h_neg_val_nat : (-t).val = T - t.val := ZMod.val_neg_of_ne_zero (a := t)\n167 |     have h_neg_val : ((-t).val : ℤ) = (T : ℤ) - (t.val : ℤ) := by\n168 |       have h_le : t.val ≤ T := Nat.le_of_lt h_val_lt\n169 |       simp [h_neg_val_nat, Nat.cast_sub h_le, Nat.cast_ofNat]\n170 |     split_ifs with h1 h2\n171 |     · -- Both h1 and h2 hold: ((-t).val : ℕ) ≤ (T-1)/2 and (t.val : ℕ) ≤ (T-1)/2\n172 |       -- Then (-t).val = T - t.val, and we have T - t.val ≤ (T-1)/2\n173 |       -- But if t.val ≤ (T-1)/2 then T - t.val > (T-1)/2, contradiction\n174 |       have : T - t.val > (T-1)/2 := by omega\n175 |       rw [h_neg_val_nat] at h1\n176 |       omega\n177 |     · -- h1 true, h2 false: ((-t).val : ℕ) ≤ (T-1)/2, (t.val : ℕ) > (T-1)/2\n178 |       rw [h_neg_val_nat] at h1\n179 |       omega\n180 |     · -- h1 false, h2 true: ((-t).val : ℕ) > (T-1)/2, (t.val : ℕ) ≤ (T-1)/2\n181 |       omega\n182 |     · -- Both false\n183 |       omega\n184 | \n185 | /--\n186 | The set of lattice sites with positive signed time (strictly positive).\n187 | -/\n188 | noncomputable def positiveSites (T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] : Finset (PeriodicSite T L) :=\n189 |   Finset.filter (λ n => signedTime T n.time > 0) Finset.univ\n190 | \n191 | /--\n192 | The set of lattice sites with negative signed time.\n193 | -/\n194 | noncomputable def negativeSites (T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] : Finset (PeriodicSite T L) :=\n195 |   Finset.filter (λ n => signedTime T n.time < 0) Finset.univ\n196 | \n197 | /--\n198 | The set of lattice sites at the time interface (signed time = 0).\n199 | -/\n\n... 1327 more line(s) below (use offset=200 to continue reading)","timestamp":1784083843,"token_count":794,"tool_call_id":"call_00_e2s6EB5dl67uXo21auyx7127","full_token_estimate":596,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":1526,"byte_count":94678,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":11,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":38,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1784083877,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\": \"wilsonActionFinite_reflection_invariant\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"call_00_xLLWiBqYoEqy3SLPrvCd8642","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\": \"lemma.*wilsonActionFinite.*reflect\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"call_01_pEV7CvrrbweW5RJSSjTX7729","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":2640,"reasoning_content":"OK, let me take a step back and think about what the correct approach should be.\n\nI need to understand the math better. Let me look at how the standard OS reflection positivity works for gauge theories.\n\nIn the OS framework for lattice gauge theory:\n1. The lattice is split into positive time (t > 0), negative time (t < 0), and time 0 (interface).\n2. The action S_W = S⁺ + S⁻ + S_int where S⁺ uses plaquettes in the positive region, S⁻ uses plaquettes in the negative region, and S_int uses plaquettes crossing the interface.\n3. The transfer matrix T is defined on functions on the interface configuration space (or on the positive+interface configuration space) by integrating over negative-time links.\n4. For any function F supported on positive+interface links, we have:\n   ∫ F(U)·F(θU)·exp(-S_W) dμ₀ = ⟨F, T·F⟩ ≥ 0\n\nBut the issue in our code is that G(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2) depends on f, which is a function of ALL links.\n\nWait, but maybe I need to look at this differently. The function G is defined this way so that:\nG(U)·G(θU) = f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U))\n\nThis is an algebraic identity that's proved in the code. So:\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)\n\nNow, the transfer matrix identity that we want is:\n\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ∫ G_plus(u)·(T G_plus)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nBut as I've argued, this is false for general f because G depends on negative links through f, while G_plus sets negative links to 1.\n\nLet me think about what happens if we DON'T go through the transfer matrix approach, and instead prove the non-negativity directly.\n\nThe integral is:\n∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)\n\nSince S_W(U) = S_W(θU) (action is reflection invariant), and the Haar measure is invariant under reflection, we have:\n\n∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U) = ∫ f(θU)·f(U)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)\n\nSo the integral is symmetric in U ↔ θU.\n\nConsider the operator K on L²(μ₀) defined by:\n(KF)(U) = ∫ F(V)·exp(-β·S_W(U))δ(U, θV) dμ₀(V)\n\nNo, this doesn't make sense.\n\nLet me think about this from a linear algebra perspective. Let's define the probability measure:\ndν(U) = (1/Z)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)\n\nThen the expectation is:\n⟨f·θf⟩ = ∫ f(U)·f(θU) dν(U)\n\nDefine the operator Θ on L²(ν) by (Θf)(U) = f(θU). Then Θ is a unitary operator (since θ is involutive and measure-preserving), and Θ² = I.\n\nThe reflection positivity condition is that Θ is a positive operator, i.e., ⟨f, Θf⟩ ≥ 0.\n\nHmm, but ⟨f, Θf⟩ = ∫ f(U)·f(θU) dν(U) which is exactly what we need to show is ≥ 0.\n\nIf Θ is a positive operator, then this holds. And the standard way to show Θ is positive is through the transfer matrix.\n\nLet me think about whether there's a simpler way.\n\nActually, the key insight from the OS construction is:\n\nDefine the operator T on L²(μ⁺ × μ⁰) by:\n(T ψ)(U⁺, U⁰) = ∫ ψ(U⁻, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰))·exp(-β·(S_OS⁻(U⁻) + S_OS⁺(U⁺))/2) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nWait, this is getting closer to the correct formula. Let me define:\n\nG⁺(U⁺, U⁰) = exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺)/2)·g⁺(U⁺, U⁰) for some g⁺\n\nThen:\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g⁺(U⁺, U⁰)·(T g⁺)(U⁺, U⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nBut this requires G to factor as G(U) = g⁺(U⁺, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺)/2) where g⁺ doesn't depend on U⁻. And G(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2).\n\nSo g⁺(U⁺, U⁰) = f(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺)/2)·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)/2) which STILL depends on U⁻ through f and S_OS_int.\n\nSo the factorization doesn't work in general.\n\nUnless... the transfer matrix T is defined as:\n(T g⁺)(U⁺, U⁰) = ∫ g⁺(U⁻, U⁰)·exp(-β·(S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) + S_OS⁻(U⁻) + S_OS⁺(U⁺))/2) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nBut then the identity would be more complex.\n\nActually wait. Let me re-think. The original reflection positivity lemma is about the EXPECTATION, not the numerator directly:\n\n⟨f·θf⟩ = (1/Z) ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)\n\nThe proof by OS says: write this as:\n\n⟨f·θf⟩ = ∫ f(U)·f(θU) dν(U) = (1/Z) ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U)\n\nwhere G(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2)\n\nNow, define:\nH(U⁺, U⁰) = G(U⁺, ?, U⁰)\n\nBut G still depends on U⁻ through f.\n\nOK I think I'm going in circles. Let me try a completely different approach.\n\nWhat if the `transferMatrix_identity` is not about G_plus at all, but about a different function? Let me re-read:\n\n```lean4\nlemma transferMatrix_identity (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ) :\n    (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n      G N T L hT β f (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg) *\n      G N T L hT β f (reflectLinkVariable N (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg))\n      ∂ productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =\n    (∫ (cfg : PosInterfaceConfig N T L),\n      G_plus N T L hT β f cfg * (transferMatrix N T L β (G_plus N T L hT β f) cfg)\n      ∂ haarMeasurePosInterface N T L) := ...\n```\n\nHmm, what if the identity is actually false as stated, but we can prove a DIFFERENT identity that gives the non-negativity? \n\nActually, let me look at `transferMatrix_positive` again:\n\n```lean4\nlemma transferMatrix_positive (ψ : PosInterfaceConfig N T L → ℝ) :\n    0 ≤ (∫ (cfg : PosInterfaceConfig N T L),\n      ψ cfg * (transferMatrix N T L β ψ) cfg\n      ∂ haarMeasurePosInterface N T L) := ...\n```\n\nThe proof shows that `(T ψ)(cfg) = ψ(cfg)·kernel(cfg)` where kernel ≥ 0, so the integrand is ψ(cfg)²·kernel(cfg) ≥ 0.\n\nNow, `integral_G_thetaG_nonneg` uses this by noting that:\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ G_plus(u)·(T G_plus)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) ≥ 0\n\nBut I've shown this identity is false. So we need a different approach.\n\nLet me think about what could be correct. Instead of using `G_plus`, maybe we should use a different function:\n\nLet ψ(u) = G_plus(u) (or some modification).\n\nActually, what if we define:\nH(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U)/2)\n\nand then define:\nD(U) = exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2)·H(U)\n\nWait, this doesn't help.\n\nLet me try yet another angle. What if the correct identity is:\n\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ∫ ψ(U⁺, U⁰)·(T' ψ)(U⁺, U⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nwhere ψ(U⁺, U⁰) = G(U⁺, 𝟙, U⁰) (same as G_plus) and T' is defined by:\n\n(T' ψ)(U⁺, U⁰) = ∫ ψ(U⁻, U⁰)·G(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)/G(U⁺, 𝟙, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)/2)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺)/2) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nThis is contrived and not useful.\n\nOK, I think the fundamental issue is that the `transferMatrix_identity` as stated is wrong, but the overall goal of proving `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` is still achievable.\n\nLet me consider a direct approach:\n\n**Approach**: Write the integral as a sum of squares by using the Fourier expansion on SU(N) (Peter-Weyl theorem).\n\nActually, the Peter-Weyl approach is mentioned in the comments as the \"proper\" mathematical proof. The transfer matrix approach is a shortcut that avoids Peter-Weyl, but as I've identified, it has issues.\n\nWait, let me re-read the comments in the code more carefully. The comment in `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` says:\n\n```\n-- MATHEMATICAL ARGUMENT (Osterwalder–Seiler 1979, §3):\n-- The product Haar measure μ₀ on the finite periodic lattice factorises\n-- as μ₀ = μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁻ ⊗ μ⁰.\n-- ...\n-- The operator T is POSITIVE because the integral kernel\n-- K(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) = exp(-β · S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) is a positive-definite\n-- function on (SU(N)ⁿ × SU(N)ⁿ). This follows from the Peter–Weyl theorem\n-- on SU(N).\n```\n\nSo the original approach was:\n1. Show the identity with the transfer matrix (which involves G_plus)\n2. Show T is positive using Peter-Weyl\n\nBut the \"transferMatrix_positive\" proof that was recently completed takes a different approach: it shows T is a multiplication operator by a positive kernel (because restrict_merge_id reduces Tψ = ψ·kernel). This means T is positive trivially, but it also means T is just multiplication, not a proper \"transfer matrix\" that creates entanglement.\n\nWait, that's the issue! The `transferMatrix_positive` proof is TOO trivial because it shows T is a multiplication operator, which means T doesn't actually couple different configurations. This is because `restrict_merge_id` shows that `restrictPosInterface ∘ mergeConfigurations = id`, which means the function ψ in the integrand doesn't depend on the U⁻ variable, so the integral factorizes.\n\nBut this triviality is because of how `restrictPosInterface` and `mergeConfigurations` are defined. The function ψ is applied to `restrictPosInterface(mergeConfigurations(U⁻, u))` which by `restrict_merge_id` equals `u` (independent of U⁻). So the integral over U⁻ doesn't affect ψ.\n\nThis means the transfer matrix as currently defined doesn't create a genuine \"transfer\" — it's just a multiplication operator. This is mathematically correct but trivial.\n\nNow, for the identity to hold, we'd need:\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ G_plus(u)²·(∫ exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nwhere G_plus(u) = G(U⁺, 𝟙, U⁰).\n\nBut the LHS is:\n∫ f(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)·f(θU⁺,θU⁻,θU⁰)·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(U⁺)+S_OS⁻(U⁻)+S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰))) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰\n\nThese are clearly not equal unless f(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰) = f(U⁺,𝟙,U⁰) for all U⁻ (i.e., f doesn't depend on negative links).\n\nSo the identity is wrong, and we need a different approach.\n\nLet me think about what a correct statement would be.\n\nActually, looking at this from a different perspective: maybe the function `G` is defined incorrectly, and the whole approach needs adjustment.\n\nLet me re-read the goal:\n```\ngibbsExpectation N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n      (λ U => f U * reflectObservable N f U) ≥ 0\n```\n\nThis is:\n(1/Z) ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0\n\nSince Z > 0, we need to show the numerator N = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0.\n\nNow, let's make a change of variables. The Haar measure μ₀ is reflection-invariant (since Haar measure on SU(N) is invariant under inversion and the product measure is invariant under any permutation of factors). So:\n\nLet me define a transformation on the full configuration space. Let's pair up each link with its reflected counterpart.\n\nActually, here's a simpler idea. For a configuration U, define:\n\nA(U) = f(U)·√(exp(-β·S_W(U)))\n\nThen:\nf(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) = f(U)·√(exp(-β·S_W(U)))·f(θU)·√(exp(-β·S_W(θU)))\n\n= A(U)·A(θU)\n\nSince S_W(U) = S_W(θU) (proved as `wilsonActionFinite_reflection_invariant`).\n\nWait, is this lemma proved? Let me check.","turn":12,"is_prune_marker":false}
