ctation f = (∫ f(U)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)) / Z\n\nWait, that's not quite right. The `gibbsExpectation` takes f as a function on LinkVariable, not on FiniteLinkConfig. And inside, it extends the FiniteLinkConfig to a full LinkVariable.\n\nFor sites = univ, extendLinkVariable(N, Λ, univ, U) = the LinkVariable corresponding to U (since all sites are in univ). So it IS the identity.\n\nSo:\ngibbsExpectation(λ U => f(U)·f(θU)) = (∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)) / Z\n\nThis is what I was computing. My counterexample stands.\n\nAlright, so the lemma is false as stated. We need to either:\n1. Correct the lemma to require f to be supported on positive+interface links\n2. Or prove a weaker statement\n\nLet me think about what the correct lemma should be. The standard OS theorem says:\n\nFor any function F that depends only on gauge fields in the positive-time region (including the interface), we have:\n\n⟨F*, θF⟩ ≥ 0\n\nwhere θF(U) = F(θU).\n\nIf we take F = f (real-valued), then this says ⟨f, θf⟩ ≥ 0 where f depends only on positive-time links.\n\nSo the correct lemma should be:\n```\nlemma gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\n    (hT : Odd T) (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ)\n    (hf_supported : ... ) :  -- f depends only on positive+interface links\n    0 ≤ gibbsExpectation N β (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))\n      (λ U => f U * reflectObservable N f U) := ...\n```\n\nBut what does \"depends only on positive+interface links\" mean? It means that for any two configurations U and V that agree on all positive and interface links, f(U) = f(V).\n\nEquivalently, f factors through the restriction map:\nf = g ∘ restrictPosInterface\n\nfor some function g on PosInterfaceConfig.\n\nNow, under this hypothesis, we have:\nf(U) = g(U⁺, U⁰) (independent of U⁻)\nf(θU) = g(θU restricted to positive+interface links)\n\nBut θU restricted to positive+interface links... θ maps positive to negative and vice versa, and preserves interface. So:\n(θU) restricted to positive+interface links = (θU)⁺ = θ⁻(U⁻) — the reflection of the negative links to the positive side.\n\nWait, I need to think about this more carefully. For a positive site n, (θU)(n, μ) depends on U at θ(n), which is a negative site. So (θU) at positive sites involves the negative links of U.\n\nSo f(θU) depends on the negative links of U. But f depends only on positive+interface links. So f(θU) = g(restriction of θU to positive+interface links) = g(θ(U⁻, U⁰)).\n\nSo the integrand becomes:\nf(U)·f(θU) = g(U⁺, U⁰)·g(θ(U⁻, U⁰))\n\nAnd the integral is:\n∫ g(U⁺, U⁰)·g(θ(U⁻, U⁰))·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀(U)\n\nUsing the measure factorization and action decomposition:\n\n= ∫ g(U⁺, U⁰)·g(θ(U⁻, U⁰))·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(U⁺) + S_OS⁻(U⁻) + S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰))) dμ⁺dμ⁻dμ⁰\n\nNow, this IS of the form ∫ G_plus(u)·(T G_plus)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) where G_plus = g·exp(-β·S_OS⁺/2)·Something and T is the transfer matrix.\n\nActually, for f supported on positive+interface links, the factorization works because f doesn't depend on U⁻. Let me work this out.\n\nf(U) = g(U⁺, U⁰) for some g.\n\nDefine G(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2)\n\nSince f doesn't depend on U⁻, but S_OS_int does (it involves negative links in interface plaquettes), G still depends on U⁻ through S_OS_int.\n\nSo even with the hypothesis that f is supported on positive+interface links, G still depends on U⁻ through S_OS_int.\n\nHmm. So we still have the same issue.\n\nUnless... we define the transfer matrix differently. The OS transfer matrix should integrate over ALL links (both positive and negative), not just negative ones.\n\nActually, let me re-read the OS paper's construction. The transfer matrix acts on functions of the INTERFACE links (time 0), not on functions of positive+interface links.\n\nIn the OS construction:\n- The positive-time region consists of links at positive time AND the interface links.\n- A function F on the positive-time region can be written as F = A·B where A depends on positive-time-only links and B depends on interface links.\n- The reflection θ maps positive-time links to negative-time links and preserves interface links.\n- The transfer matrix T is defined on functions of the interface configuration.\n- The identity is: ⟨F, θF⟩ = ⟨B, T·B⟩ for F = A·B where A is \"created\" by the dynamics from B.\n\nThis is getting quite complex. The key point is that the transfer matrix in the standard OS construction is NOT the same as the one defined in this code.\n\nOK, let me take a step back and think about what to do PRACTICALLY.\n\nGiven the situation:\n1. The lemma `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` as stated (for all f) is false.\n2. The `transferMatrix_identity` is incorrect (for the same reason).\n3. The `transferMatrix_positive` is correct but trivial.\n\nWhat should I do? I think the best approach is to:\n1. Correct the lemma to require f to be supported on positive+interface links.\n2. Provide a correct proof for the corrected lemma using the OS transfer matrix approach (not the flawed one in the code).\n\nBut the transfer matrix approach in the code is flawed even for this corrected lemma, because G_plus still involves S_OS_int evaluated at negative links = 1.\n\nSo I need a different approach for the corrected lemma.\n\nActually, let me think about this from scratch. For f supported on positive+interface links:\n- f(U) = g(restrictPosInterface(U)) for some g: PosInterfaceConfig → ℝ\n\nThe integrand is:\nf(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U))\n\n= g(U⁺, U⁰)·g(restrictPosInterface(θU))·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(U⁺) + S_OS⁻(U⁻) + S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)))\n\nNow, restrictPosInterface(θU):\n(θU) restricted to positive+interface sites. For a positive site n:\n(θU)(n) = U(θ(n)) with orientation handling\nFor an interface site n (time=0):\n(θU)(n) = U(n) with orientation handling (inverse for time links)\n\nSo restrictPosInterface(θU) depends on U⁻ (for positive sites) and U⁰ (for interface sites).\n\nLet's denote:\n- U⁺⁰ = (U⁺, U⁰) — the positive+interface config\n- U⁻ — the negative config\n- U⁰ — the interface config\n\nThen:\ng(U⁺, U⁰) = g(U⁺, U⁰) — depends on U⁺ and U⁰\ng(restrictPosInterface(θU)) = g(θ⁻(U⁻), θ⁰(U⁰)) — depends on U⁻ and U⁰\n\nAnd:\nS_OS⁺(U⁺) — depends on U⁺\nS_OS⁻(U⁻) — depends on U⁻\nS_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) — depends on U⁺, U⁻, U⁰\n\nSo the integrand is:\ng(U⁺, U⁰)·g(θ⁻U⁻, θ⁰U⁰)·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(U⁺) + S_OS⁻(U⁻) + S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)))\n\nNow, we define:\nG⁺(U⁺, U⁰) = g(U⁺, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺)/2)·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,?,U⁰)/2)\n\nBut S_OS_int depends on U⁻ too, so we can't separate it cleanly.\n\nHmm, but the standard OS construction handles this. Let me think about the OS transfer matrix differently.\n\nIn the OS construction, the transfer matrix is an operator on L²(μ⁰) (the Hilbert space of functions on the interface configuration). It's defined by:\n\n(T ψ)(U⁰) = ∫ ψ(θ⁻¹U⁻)·exp(-β·(S_OS⁻(U⁻) + S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)/2)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nWait, this still depends on U⁺.\n\nActually, the transfer matrix in the OS construction is defined via a conditional expectation. For a function F on the positive-time configuration space (positive links + interface links), the conditional expectation onto the interface is:\n\n(E[F | interface])(U⁰) = ∫ F(U⁺, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺)) dμ⁺(U⁺) / Z⁺(U⁰)\n\nwhere Z⁺ is a normalization factor.\n\nAnd the transfer matrix is:\n(T ψ)(U⁰) = ∫ ψ(θ⁻¹U⁻)·exp(-β·(S_OS⁻(U⁻) + S_OS_int(·,U⁻,U⁰)/2)) dμ⁻(U⁻) / Z⁻(U⁰)\n\nThe key identity is then that for F = G⁺(U⁺)·ψ(U⁰):\n⟨F, θF⟩ = ∫ ψ*(U⁰)·(T ψ)(U⁰) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nwhere (θF)(U) = F(θU) = G⁺(θ⁻U⁻)·ψ(θ⁰U⁰).\n\nThis is the standard OS transfer matrix formalism. The proof uses the fact that the measure factorizes and the action decomposes into positive, negative, and interface parts.\n\nIn our case, G(U) takes the specific form:\nG(U) = f(U)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U)/2)\n\nIf f = g∘restrictPosInterface (supported on positive+interface), then:\nG(U) = g(U⁺, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)/2)\n\nNow, this still depends on U⁻ through S_OS_int. So we can't factor G(U) = G⁺(U⁺, U⁰)·G⁻(U⁻) for some G⁺, G⁻.\n\nThe OS construction handles this by noting that the TRNASFER MATRIX includes the integral over U⁻, and the factorization works because:\n\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ G⁺(U⁺, U⁰)·(T G⁺)(U⁺, U⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nwhere G⁺ is some function derived from G and T is defined appropriately.\n\nThe key is that T must INCLUDE the dependence on S_OS_int that involves negative links. In the code, the current T includes S_OS_int but with the full config, which IS correct:\n\n(T ψ)(U⁺⁰) = ∫ ψ(restrict(merge(U⁻, U⁺⁰)))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(extendToFull(U⁻, U⁺⁰))) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nThis T, applied to ψ = G_plus, gives:\n(T G_plus)(U⁺⁰) = G_plus(U⁺⁰)·∫ exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nAnd the RHS of the identity is:\n∫ G_plus(U⁺⁰)·(T G_plus)(U⁺⁰) dμ⁺⁰(U⁺⁰)\n= ∫ G_plus(U⁺⁰)²·∫ exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻) dμ⁺⁰(U⁺⁰)\n\nNow, the LHS is:\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U)\n= ∫ G(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)·G(θU⁺, θU⁻, θU⁰) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰\n\nIf f depends only on U⁺⁰:\nG(U) = f(U⁺, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)/2)\n\nG(θU) = f(θU⁻, θU⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(θU⁻))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)/2)\n       = f(θU⁻, θU⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁻(U⁻))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)/2)\n\nSo G(U)·G(θU) = f(U⁺,U⁰)·f(θU⁻,θU⁰)·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(U⁺)+S_OS⁻(U⁻)+S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)))\n\nNow, the RHS:\nG_plus(U⁺⁰) = G(U⁺, 𝟙, U⁰) = f(U⁺,U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,𝟙,U⁰)/2)\n\n(T G_plus)(U⁺⁰) = G_plus(U⁺⁰)·∫ exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nSo RHS = ∫ f(U⁺,U⁰)²·exp(-β·(2·S_OS⁺(U⁺)+S_OS_int(U⁺,𝟙,U⁰)))·∫ exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)) dμ⁻ dμ⁺⁰\n\nLHS = ∫ f(U⁺,U⁰)·f(θU⁻,θU⁰)·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(U⁺)+S_OS⁻(U⁻)+S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰))) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰\n\nThese are DIFFERENT even when f depends only on U⁺⁰ because:\n1. G_plus uses S_OS_int(U⁺,𝟙,U⁰) while the LHS uses S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)\n2. The LHS has f(θU⁻,θU⁰) while the RHS has f(U⁺,U⁰)\n\nSo the identity is still wrong! This is because G_plus sets negative links to 1, which doesn't match the actual U⁻ in the full measure.\n\nOK, I think the fundamental problem is that the transfer matrix in the code is defined to act on functions of PosInterfaceConfig (positive + interface links), and it integrates over negative links. But the function G_plus is defined by setting negative links to 1 in the Boltzmann factors, which is not the same as integrating them out.\n\nThe correct definition would be:\n\nG_plus_effective(U⁺, U⁰) = ∫ f(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)/2) dμ⁻(U⁻) / something\n\nBut this would require integrating out the negative links IN THE DEFINITION of G_plus, not in the transfer matrix.\n\nI think the correct OS approach is:\n1. Define the transfer matrix T on functions of INTERFACE config (time 0 only), not positive+interface.\n2. Define G_0(U⁰) as the restriction of G to the interface (integrating out all other links).\n3. The identity is: ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ G_0(U⁰)·(T G_0)(U⁰) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n\nBut this is a complex construction.\n\nOK, I've spent a lot of time analyzing the issue. Let me now take action. I think the most productive thing is to:\n\n1. Acknowledge that the current approach is flawed\n2. Write a document explaining the issue\n3. Provide guidance on how to fix it\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider one more time. Maybe I'm wrong about S_OS_int depending on negative links.\n\nInterface plaquettes are those where NOT all corners are positive AND NOT all corners are negative. The links in these plaquettes are at sites that may be positive, negative, or interface.\n\nBut actually, for the specific plaquettes that are interface plaquettes, the four corners must span the time interface. This means that in the time direction, some corners have time > 0 and some have time < 0.\n\nFor a plaquette in the (0, j) plane (time + spatial direction), the corners are:\nn, n+e_0, n+e_0+e_j, n+e_j\n\nIf n has positive time and n+e_0 has negative time (wrapping around in ZMod T), then:\n- n is positive\n- n+e_0 is negative\n- n+e_0+e_j is negative\n- n+e_j is positive\n\nThe links involved are:\n- U(n, 0) — from n (positive) to n+e_0 (negative) — this is a time-direction link at a POSITIVE site\n- U(n+e_0, j) — from n+e_0 (negative) to n+e_0+e_j (negative) — this is a spatial link at a NEGATIVE site\n- U(n+e_0+e_j, 0)⁻¹ — from n+e_0+e_j (negative) to n+e_j (positive) — this is a time-direction link at a NEGATIVE site\n- U(n+e_j, j)⁻¹ — from n+e_j (positive) to n (positive) — this is a spatial link at a POSITIVE site\n\nSo the interface plaquette involves links at both positive and negative sites!\n\nThis means S_OS_int DOES depend on negative links, confirming my analysis.\n\nSo the current approach in the code cannot work. We need a fundamentally different approach.\n\nLet me now plan what to do.\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider. Maybe the goal of `transferMatrix_identity` is not what I think it is. Let me re-read it:\n\n```lean4\nlemma transferMatrix_identity (f : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L) → ℝ) :\n    (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n      G N T L hT β f (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg) *\n      G N T L hT β f (reflectLinkVariable N (extendLinkVariable N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L)) cfg))\n      ∂ productHaarMeasure N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =\n    (∫ (cfg : PosInterfaceConfig N T L),\n      G_plus N T L hT β f cfg * (transferMatrix N T L β (G_plus N T L hT β f) cfg)\n      ∂ haarMeasurePosInterface N T L) := ...\n```\n\nThe LHS integrates over the full config space and uses G. The RHS integrates over positive+interface config space and uses G_plus and the transfer matrix.\n\nI've shown this is false. So what should we do?\n\nI think the pragmatic approach is:\n1. Add a hypothesis to `gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` that `f` is supported on positive+interface links.\n2. Provide a correct proof using the measure factorization and the identity:\n   ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀ = ∫ f(U⁺,U⁰)·f(θ⁻U⁻,θ⁰U⁰)·exp(-β·(...)) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰\n   \n   Then use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality or the positivity of the transfer matrix.\n\nActually, let me think about a much simpler approach. For f supported on positive+interface links:\n\nf(U) = g(U⁺, U⁰) for some g.\n\nThen:\nf(θU) = g(restrictPosInterface(θU))\n\nBut restrictPosInterface(θU) at a positive site n gives θU(n) which depends on U(θ(n)). Since θ(n) is a negative site, this depends on U⁻.\n\nAnd at an interface site n (time=0), θU(n) depends on U(n) (since θ(n) = n).\n\nSo f(θU) = g(θ⁻(U⁻), θ⁰(U⁰)) where θ⁻ maps negative configs to positive configs and θ⁰ acts on interface configs.\n\nNow, the integral is:\n∫ g(U⁺, U⁰)·g(θ⁻(U⁻), θ⁰(U⁰))·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(U⁺)+S_OS⁻(U⁻)+S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰))) dμ⁺dμ⁻dμ⁰\n\nLet me make a change of variables: V⁺ = U⁺, V⁰ = U⁰, V⁻ = θ⁻(U⁻).\n\nThen the integral becomes... hmm, but θ⁻ is a bijection on the negative config space, and the Haar measure is invariant under this bijection (since reflection is a bijection on links). So:\n\n= ∫ g(V⁺, V⁰)·g(V⁻, θ⁰(V⁰))·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(V⁺)+S_OS⁻(θ⁻¹(V⁻))+S_OS_int(V⁺,θ⁻¹(V⁻),V⁰))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁻(V⁻) dμ⁰(V⁰)\n\nUsing S_OS⁻(θ⁻¹(V⁻)) = S_OS⁺(V⁻) (by the lemma):\n= ∫ g(V⁺, V⁰)·g(V⁻, θ⁰(V⁰))·exp(-β·(S_OS⁺(V⁺)+S_OS⁺(V⁻)+S_OS_int(V⁺,θ⁻¹(V⁻),V⁰))) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰\n\nNow, define H(V⁺, V⁰) = g(V⁺, V⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(V⁺)/2).\n\nThen the integral is:\n∫ H(V⁺, V⁰)·H(V⁻, θ⁰(V⁰))·exp(-β·(S_OS_int(V⁺,θ⁻¹(V⁻),V⁰))) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰\n\nWait, this is getting messy. Let me try a different approach.\n\nActually, the key insight of OS is that we can DEFINE the inner product:\n⟨U⁺⁰, V⁺⁰⟩ = ∫ exp(-β·S_OS_int(merge(U⁺⁰, U⁻, V⁰))) dμ⁻(U⁻) \n\nNo, this is getting nowhere.\n\nLet me look at this from the perspective of the OS paper. The paper defines the transfer matrix K as follows:\n\n(Kf)(x) = ∫ f(y) k(x,y) dy\n\nwhere k(x,y) is the integral kernel that comes from the action of the \"time translation\" operator.\n\nFor lattice gauge theory, the transfer matrix is:\n(T f)(U⁰) = ∫ f(U⁻)·exp(-S_int(U⁰, U⁻)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nwhere U⁻ are the link variables at negative time and S_int is the interaction across the interface.\n\nThen the reflection positivity identity is:\n∫ F(U⁺, U⁰)·F(θU⁻, θU⁰)·exp(-S_W) dμ₀ = ∫ F(U⁰)·(T F)(U⁰) dμ⁰\n\nfor any function F depending only on U⁺, U⁰.\n\nBut note: here F depends on (U⁺, U⁰) and θF depends on (U⁻, U⁰). The transfer matrix T acts on functions of U⁰ ONLY (interface), not on functions of (U⁺, U⁰).\n\nThe idea is that for any function F(U⁺, U⁰), we can write:\nF(U⁺, U⁰) = (T^{t/2} φ)(U⁰) for some φ (where t is the time to the interface)\n\nActually, I think the construction is more subtle. In the OS paper, the transfer matrix is defined by:\n(T φ)(U⁰) = ∫ φ(U⁻)·exp(-S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nWait, but T shouldn't depend on U⁺. Let me look at this again.\n\nActually, I think the correct definition is:\n\nGiven a function ψ on the INTERFACE config (time 0), define:\n(T ψ)(U⁰) = ∫ ψ(θ⁻¹(U⁻))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(path to interface)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n\nNo, this doesn't work either because S_OS_int depends on both U⁺ and U⁻.\n\nOK I think I need to read the OS paper properly to understand the construction. Let me search for it.","turn":16,"is_prune_marker":false}
