{"id":109,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\ReflectionPositivity.lean\n-- 1545 lines, 95993 bytes --\n1407 |   calc\n1408 |     (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1409 |       f (U_ext cfg) * f (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) * Real.exp (-β * S_total cfg) ∂ μ₀)\n1410 |         = (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1411 |       G (U_ext cfg) * G (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) ∂ μ₀) := by\n1412 |       rw [h_fun_eq]\n1413 |     _ ≥ 0 := by\n1414 |       -- GOAL: Prove that ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0.\n1415 |       --\n1416 |       -- This is the core of the Osterwalder–Seiler reflection positivity proof.\n1417 |       -- The integrand G·θG is NOT pointwise non-negative; the non-negativity\n1418 |       -- holds only after integration with respect to the product Haar measure.\n1419 |       --\n1420 |       -- MATHEMATICAL ARGUMENT (Osterwalder–Seiler 1979, §3):\n1421 |       --\n1422 |       -- The product Haar measure μ₀ on the finite periodic lattice factorises\n1423 |       -- as μ₀ = μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁻ ⊗ μ⁰, where μ⁺/μ⁻/μ⁰ are the Haar measures on links\n1424 |       -- in the positive-time, negative-time, and interface regions respectively.\n1425 |       --\n1426 |       -- Define the transfer matrix T acting on L²(μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁰) by:\n1427 |       --\n1428 |       --   (Tψ)(U⁺, U⁰) = ∫ ψ(U⁻, U⁰) · exp(-β · S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n1429 |       --\n1430 |       -- where S_OS_int is the interface part of the Wilson action.  The key\n1431 |       -- identity (proved algebraically above) is:\n1432 |       --\n1433 |       --   ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ⟨G⁺, T·G⁺⟩_{L²(μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁰)}\n1434 |       --\n1435 |       -- where G⁺ is the restriction of G to variables in the positive-time and\n1436 |       -- interface regions.  The operator T is POSITIVE because the integral kernel\n1437 |       -- K(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰) = exp(-β · S_OS_int(U⁺, U⁻, U⁰)) is a positive-definite\n1438 |       -- function on (SU(N)ⁿ × SU(N)ⁿ).  This follows from the Peter–Weyl theorem\n1439 |       -- on SU(N): the matrix elements of the unitary irreducible representations\n1440 |       -- form an orthonormal basis of L²(SU(N)), and the Boltzmann factor expands\n1441 |       -- as a sum of squares in this basis (OS 1979, Theorem 3.1).\n1442 |       --\n1443 |       -- Consequently, ⟨G⁺, T·G⁺⟩ ≥ 0, and the integral is non-negative.\n1444 |       --\n1445 |       -- FORMALIZATION STATUS:\n1446 |       --\n1447 |       -- The Peter–Weyl theorem for SU(N) (or more generally for compact Lie groups)\n1448 |       -- is not yet available in Mathlib.  The spectral theory of compact integral\n1449 |       -- operators on L² is also not yet formalised.  Filling this gap requires:\n1450 |       --\n1451 |       --   1. The Peter–Weyl theorem: L²(SU(N)) ≅ ⊕_{λ} V_λ ⊗ V_λ^* (as\n1452 |       --      SU(N)×SU(N)-representations), where λ runs over dominant weights.\n1453 |       --   2. The Bochner–Minlos theorem or its Lie-group analogue: positive-definite\n1454 |       --      functions correspond to measures / states on the group C*-algebra.\n1455 |       --   3. Positivity of the transfer matrix T constructed from the Wilson\n1456 |       --      interface action.\n1457 |       --\n1458 |       -- REFERENCES:\n1459 |       --   - K. Osterwalder, E. Seiler, \"Gauge Field Theories on a Lattice\"\n1460 |       --     (Ann. Phys. 110, 1978, pp 440–471), §3.\n1461 |       --   - J. Glimm, A. Jaffe, \"Quantum Physics\" (2nd ed.), §6.1.\n1462 |       --   - M. Salmhofer, \"Construction of a Higgs field\" (thesis, 1990).\n1463 |       --\n1464 |       -- Until these results are available in Mathlib, the last step remains\n1465 |       -- an open `sorry`.  The rest of the reflection positivity proof (all\n1466 |       -- algebraic identities, the action decomposition, and the reduction\n1467 |       -- to the integral of G·θG) is fully forma