{"id":127,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\docs\\gap_analysis.md\n-- 219 lines, 9593 bytes --\n  1 | # Gap Analysis: Transfer Matrix Positivity for Reflection Positivity\n  2 | \n  3 | ## Current Status\n  4 | \n  5 | The reflection positivity proof for the Wilson action on a finite periodic lattice\n  6 | (`gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` in `ReflectionPositivity.lean:1312-1448`)\n  7 | is complete except for one `sorry` at line 1448.\n  8 | \n  9 | ### What is proved (no gaps):\n 10 | \n 11 | 1. **Action decomposition**: $S_W = S^+_{OS} + S^-_{OS} + S^0_{OS}$ (OS plaquette decomposition)\n 12 |    - `total_decomposition_os_periodic`\n 13 |    - `neg_action_reflection_os_periodic`: $S^-_{OS}[U] = S^+_{OS}[\\theta U]$\n 14 |    - `interface_action_reflection_symmetric_os_periodic`: $S^0_{OS}[\\theta U] = S^0_{OS}[U]$\n 15 | \n 16 | 2. **Algebraic factorization**: $\\exp(-\\beta S_W) = G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U)$ where\n 17 |    $G(U) = f(U) \\exp(-\\beta S^+_{OS}(U)) \\exp(-\\beta S^0_{OS}(U)/2)$\n 18 |    - `h_factorization`, `h_integrand_eq`\n 19 | \n 20 | 3. **Reduction to integral**: $\\langle f \\cdot \\theta f \\rangle = \\int G(U) G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0(U)$\n 21 | \n 22 | ### The remaining gap:\n 23 | \n 24 | $$\\int_{\\text{config}} G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0(U) \\ge 0$$\n 25 | \n 26 | where $\\mu_0$ is the product Haar measure on $\\text{SU}(N)^{\\text{links}}$.\n 27 | \n 28 | ## Why This Is Non-Trivial\n 29 | \n 30 | The integrand $G(U) \\cdot G(\\theta U)$ is **not pointwise non-negative**. The\n 31 | non-negativity holds only after integration. This is the essence of reflection\n 32 | positivity: it is a global property of the measure, not a pointwise one.\n 33 | \n 34 | ## Mathematical Proof (Osterwalder-Seiler 1979, §3)\n 35 | \n 36 | ### Step 1: Measure factorization\n 37 | \n 38 | The links are partitioned into three sets:\n 39 | - $L^+$: links with positive signed time\n 40 | - $L^-$: links with negative signed time\n 41 | - $L^0$: links at the time interface (signed time = 0)\n 42 | \n 43 | The product Haar measure factorizes:\n 44 | $$\\mu_0 = \\mu^+ \\otimes \\mu^- \\otimes \\mu^0$$\n 45 | \n 46 | ### Step 2: Transfer matrix\n 47 | \n 48 | Define the transfer matrix $T$ acting on $L^2(\\mu^+ \\otimes \\mu^0)$ by:\n 49 | \n 50 | $$(T\\psi)(U^+, U^0) = \\int_{U^-} \\psi(U^-, U^0) \\cdot K(U^+, U^-, U^0) \\, d\\mu^-(U^-)$$\n 51 | \n 52 | where $K(U^+, U^-, U^0) = \\exp(-\\beta \\cdot S^0_{OS}(U^+, U^-, U^0))$ is the\n 53 | interface Boltzmann factor.\n 54 | \n 55 | ### Step 3: Key identity\n 56 | \n 57 | $$\\int G(U) G(\\theta U) \\, d\\mu_0 = \\langle G^+, T G^+ \\rangle_{L^2(\\mu^+ \\otimes \\mu^0)}$$\n 58 | \n 59 | where $G^+$ is the restriction of $G$ to $L^+ \\cup L^0$.\n 60 | \n 61 | ### Step 4: Positivity of $T$\n 62 | \n 63 | The transfer matrix $T$ is a **positive operator** (self-adjoint with non-negative\n 64 | spectrum). This follows from the Peter-Weyl theorem on $\\text{SU}(N)$:\n 65 | \n 66 | 1. The kernel $K(U^+, U^-, U^0)$ is a product over interface plaquettes:\n 67 |    $$K = \\prod_{\\text{interface plaquettes } p} \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{N} \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(U_{\\partial p})\\right)$$\n 68 | \n 69 | 2. For a single plaquette, the function $\\exp(c \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g))$ on\n 70 |    $\\text{SU}(N)$ can be expanded in characters (representations):\n 71 |    $$\\exp(c \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g)) = \\sum_{\\lambda} a_\\lambda(c) \\chi_\\lambda(g)$$\n 72 |    with $a_\\lambda(c) \\ge 0$ for $c > 0$.\n 73 | \n 74 | 3. The characters $\\chi_\\lambda$ are positive-definite functions on $\\text{SU}(N)$,\n 75 |    and the tensor product of positive-definite functions is positive-definite.\n 76 | \n 77 | 4. By the Peter-Weyl theorem, the matrix elements of irreducible representations\n 78 |    form an orthonormal basis of $L^2(\\text{SU}(N))$, and any positive-definite\n 79 |    function expands with non-negative coefficients in this basis.\n 80 | \n 81 | 5. Therefore $K$ is a positive-definite kernel, $T$ is a positive operator,\n 82 |    and $\\langle G^+, T G^+ \\rangle \\ge 0$.\n 83 | \n 84 | ## Required Mathlib Infrastructure\n 85 | \n 86 | ### 1. Peter-Weyl Theorem for $\\text{SU}(N)$\n 87 | \n 88 | **Not yet in Mathlib.** The following pieces are needed:\n 89 | \n 90 | | Piece | Status in Mathlib | Notes |\n 91 | |-------|-------------------|-------|\n 92 | | Compact Lie group $\\text{SU}(N)$ | Done (`SpecialUnitary.lean`) | Topological group, compactness, Haar measure |\n 93 | | Representation theory of compact groups | Partial | `RepresentationTheory/` covers finite groups |\n 94 | | Unitary irreps of $\\text{SU}(N)$ | Not available | Requires highest weight theory |\n 95 | | Peter-Weyl theorem | Not available | $L^2(G) \\cong \\bigoplus_\\lambda V_\\lambda \\otimes V_\\lambda^*$ |\n 96 | | Characters of irreps | Partial (finite groups) | `RepresentationTheory/Character` |\n 97 | | Orthogonality of characters | Partial (finite groups) | |\n 98 | | Positive-definite functions on groups | Not available | |\n 99 | | Bochner theorem for compact groups | Not available | |\n100 | \n101 | ### 2. Transfer Matrix Framework\n102 | \n103 | **Nothing in Mathlib.** The following need to be built:\n104 | \n105 | | Component | Description |\n106 | |-----------|-------------|\n107 | | $L^2$ space on $\\text{SU}(N)^n$ | Built on existing measure theory |\n108 | | Integral operators on $L^2$ | As kernel operators |\n109 | | Positivity of operators | Spectral theory of bounded operators |\n110 | | Product measures and Fubini | Available in Mathlib |\n111 | \n112 | ### 3. Character Expansion of Wilson Action\n113 | \n114 | | Component | Description |\n115 | |-----------|-------------|\n116 | | Expansion of $\\exp(c \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g))$ in characters | Requires representation theory |\n117 | | Positivity of expansion coefficients | Known result (Osterwalder-Seiler) |\n118 | | Factorization over plaquettes | Already done algebraically |\n119 | \n120 | ## Alternative Approaches\n121 | \n122 | ### A. Heat Kernel Approach\n123 | \n124 | Instead of the character expansion, one could use the heat kernel on $\\text{SU}(N)$:\n125 | \n126 | $$\\exp\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{N} \\operatorname{Re} \\operatorname{Tr}(g)\\right) = \\int_{\\mathfrak{su}(N)} e^{\\langle X, \\log g \\rangle} \\, d\\mu_\\beta(X)$$\n127 | \n128 | where $\\mu_\\beta$ is a Gaussian measure on the Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{su}(N)$.\n129 | This represents the Boltzmann factor as the Fourier transform of a positive measure,\n130 | which is automatically positive-definite.\n131 | \n132 | **Status in Mathlib**: \n133 | - Gaussian measures: Available (`Mathlib/MeasureTheory/`)\n134 | - Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{su}(N)$: Defined as `LieAlgebraSU` in `SpecialUnitary.lean`\n135 | - Matrix exponential: Not available\n136 | - Fourier transform on Lie groups: Not available\n137 | \n138 | ### B. Reflection Positivity per Plaquette\n139 | \n140 | One could attempt to prove that each individual plaquette contribution is\n141 | reflection-positive, and then use the fact that the product of reflection-positive\n142 | functions is reflection-positive. This would reduce the problem to proving\n143 | reflection positivity for a single plaquette.\n144 | \n145 | **Status**: This is essentially equivalent to the character expansion approach,\n146 | since a single plaquette is the fundamental building block.\n147 | \n148 | ### C. Finite Lattice Spectral Argument\n149 | \n150 | For a strictly finite lattice, the transfer matrix is a finite-rank operator\n151 | (acting on a finite-dimensional space if we discretize the group... but SU(N) is\n152 | continuous, so L² is infinite-dimensional). This approach doesn't avoid the\n153 | Peter-Weyl theorem.\n154 | \n155 | ## Current Status (Updated)\n156 | \n157 | ### Already Done:\n158 | \n159 | 1. **Transfer matrix definition** (`TransferMatrix.lean`): Defined `mergeConfigurations`\n160 |    (combining negative and positive+interface configurations into a full config), \n161 |    `transferMatrix` (integral operator), and `interfaceKernel`.\n162 | \n163 | 2. **Positive-definite framework** (`PositiveDefinite.lean`): New file defining\n164 |    `PositiveDefinite φ` as a predicate on group functions, with basic properties\n165 |    (sums, scalar multiples, pointwise limits). The lemma `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite`\n166 |    states the key analytic fact: exp(c Re Tr(g)) is positive-definite for c > 0,\n167 |    depending on the Peter-Weyl theorem.\n168 | \n169 | ### Remaining Steps:\n170 | \n171 | ### Short-term (measure-theoretic bookkeeping, no representation theory):\n172 | \n173 | 1. **Prove `measure_factorization`**: Show that the product Haar measure on all links\n174 |    factorizes as the tensor product of measures on positive, negative, and interface\n175 |    links. This uses a bijection of the index sets and `MeasureTheory.Measure.pi_congr`.\n176 | \n177 | 2. **Prove `transferMatrix_identity`**: Show the key identity\n178 |    ∫ G·θG dμ₀ = ⟨G⁺, T G⁺⟩ using Fubini's theorem and the reflection identities.\n179 |    This is purely measure-theoretic once the factorization is established.\n180 | \n181 | ### Medium-term (requires representation theory):\n182 | \n183 | 3. **Prove `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite`**: Show that exp(c Re Tr(g)) is\n184 |    positive-definite on SU(N) for c > 0. This is the core analytic fact.\n185 |    Approach: Peter-Weyl theorem → character expansion with non-negative coefficients.\n186 | \n187 | 4. **Prove `transferMatrix_positive`**: Show T is a positive operator.\n188 |    Follows from (3) plus the fact that products of positive-definite functions\n189 |    are positive-definite.\n190 | \n191 | ### Long-term (upstream to Mathlib):\n192 | \n193 | 1. **Peter-Weyl theorem** for compact Lie groups\n194 | 2. **Character theory** for compact (not just finite) groups\n195 | 3. **Positive-definite functions** and Bochner's theorem for compact groups\n196 | \n197 | 1. K. Osterwalder, E. Seiler, \"Gauge Field Theories on a Lattice\"\n198 |    (Ann. Phys. 110, 1978, pp 440–471), §3.\n199 |    - The original proof of reflection positivity for lattice gauge theories.\n200 |    - Uses the character expansion and Peter-Weyl theorem.\n201 | \n202 | 2. M. Lüscher, \"General Proof of Osterwalder-Schrader Positivity for the\n203 |    Wilson Action\" (Commun. Math. Phys. 113, 1987, pp 311–325).\n204 |    - Alternative proof using explicit transfer matrix construction.\n205 |    - Does not require fermions.\n206 | \n207 | 3. P. Menotti, A. Pelissetto, \"General Proof of Osterwalder-Schrader Positivity\n208 |    for the Wilson Action\" (Commun. Math. Phys. 113, 1987, pp 369–373).\n209 |    - Extension to reflections about planes containing sites.\n210 | \n211 | 4. J. Glimm, A. Jaffe, \"Quantum Physics\" (2nd ed.), §6.1.\n212 |    - Textbook treatment of reflection positivity.\n213 | \n214 | 5. M. Salmhofer, \"Construction of a Higgs field\" (thesis, 1990).\n215 |    - Detailed treatment of the transfer matrix for lattice gauge theories.\n216 | \n217 | 6. T. Bröcker, T. tom Dieck, \"Representations of Compact Lie Groups\"\n218 |    (Springer, 1985).\n219 |    - Standard reference for the Peter-Weyl theorem.\n","timestamp":1784084442,"token_count":3424,"tool_call_id":"call_00_Mlna2Q11iANjqzni5J3J8151","full_token_estimate":2533,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"docs/gap_analysis.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":219,"byte_count":9593,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":54,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":128,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1784084447,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\": \"docs/gap_analysis.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 8, \"new_text\": \"# Gap Analysis: Correct Reflection Positivity Proof\\n\\n## Current Status\\n\\nThe reflection positivity proof for the Wilson action on a finite periodic lattice\\n(`gibbsExpectationPeriodic_reflection_positive` in `ReflectionPositivity.lean`)\\nis complete except for the final positivity step (the integral of G·θG).\\n\\n### ⚠️ Important Correction (2025-06-28)\\n\\nPrevious work on the transfer matrix (`TransferMatrix.lean`) contained a\\n**mathematical error**.  The lemma `transferMatrix_identity` claimed:\\n\\n    ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ∫ G_plus(u)·(T G_plus)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\\n\\nThis is **incorrect** because:\\n- `G_plus` evaluates G at configs where negative links are set to 1, while the\\n  LHS uses the actual negative links.\\n- `transferMatrix_positive` reduces T to a multiplication operator (via\\n  `restrict_merge_id`), which trivializes the transfer matrix.\\n\\nThe correct hypothesis for the reflection positivity lemma is now enforced by\\n`dependsOnlyOnPosInterface N T L f` (f depends only on positive+interface links).\\nA correct transfer matrix with a nontrivial kernel needs to be redesigned.\\nSee `docs/found_issues.md` for details.\"}","name":"patch_lines"},"id":"call_00_3G3Yu2sIqpvovVOq7JTz0486","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":352,"reasoning_content":"The gap_analysis.md needs updating too, but it's already quite detailed. Let me just update the first section to note the found issue.","turn":55,"is_prune_marker":false}
