{"id":150,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\ReflectionPositivity.lean\n-- 1549 lines, 96144 bytes --\n1400 |     rw [h_factorization cfg]\n1401 |     ring\n1402 |   have h_fun_eq : (λ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =>\n1403 |       f (U_ext cfg) * f (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) * Real.exp (-β * S_total cfg)) =\n1404 |     (λ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))) =>\n1405 |       G (U_ext cfg) * G (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg))) := by\n1406 |     ext cfg; exact h_integrand_eq cfg\n1407 |   calc\n1408 |     (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1409 |       f (U_ext cfg) * f (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) * Real.exp (-β * S_total cfg) ∂ μ₀)\n1410 |         = (∫ (cfg : FiniteLinkConfig N (PeriodicSite T L) (Finset.univ : Finset (PeriodicSite T L))),\n1411 |       G (U_ext cfg) * G (reflectLinkVariable N (U_ext cfg)) ∂ μ₀) := by\n1412 |       rw [h_fun_eq]\n1413 |     _ ≥ 0 := by\n1414 |       -- GOAL: Prove that ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) ≥ 0.\n1415 |       --\n1416 |       -- This is the core of the Osterwalder–Seiler reflection positivity proof.\n1417 |       -- The integrand G·θG is NOT pointwise non-negative; the non-negativity\n1418 |       -- holds only after integration with respect to the product Haar measure.\n1419 |       --\n1420 |       -- The hypothesis `hf_supported : dependsOnlyOnPosInterface N T L f` guarantees\n1421 |       -- that f (and hence G) depends only on links in the positive-time and interface\n1422 |       -- (time-0) regions.  This is the correct mathematical hypothesis for the\n1423 |       -- Osterwalder-Seiler theorem.\n1424 |       --\n1425 |       -- MATHEMATICAL ARGUMENT (Osterwalder–Seiler 1979, §3):\n1426 |       --\n1427 |       -- The product Haar measure μ₀ on the finite periodic lattice factorises\n1428 |       -- as μ₀ = μ⁺ ⊗ μ⁻ ⊗ μ⁰, where μ⁺/μ⁻/μ⁰ are the Haar measures on links\n1429 |       -- in the positive-time, negative-time, and interface regions respectively.\n1430 |       --\n1431 |       -- Using `measure_factorization'` (TransferMatrix.lean) we have an explicit\n1432 |       -- measure-preserving isomorphism\n1433 |       --   μ⁺ × μ⁻ × μ⁰ ≅ μ₀.\n1434 |       --\n1435 |       -- Let G(U⁺,U⁰) = f(U⁺,U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺))·exp(-β·S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰)/2).\n1436 |       -- Note: f depends only on (U⁺,U⁰) by hypothesis, but S_OS_int depends on\n1437 |       -- U⁻ as well.\n1438 |       --\n1439 |       -- The desired identity is:\n1440 |       --\n1441 |       --   ∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀(U) = ∫ g(U⁺,U⁰)·(T g)(U⁺,U⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(U⁰)\n1442 |       --\n1443 |       -- where:\n1444 |       --   · g(U⁺,U⁰) = f(U⁺,U⁰)·exp(-β·S_OS⁺(U⁺)/2)  (depends only on pos+int links)\n1445 |       --   · (T ψ)(U⁺,U⁰) = ∫ ψ(θ⁻⁰(U⁻,U⁰))·exp(-β·(S_OS⁻(U⁻)+S_OS_int(U⁺,U⁻,U⁰))/2) dμ⁻(U⁻)\n1446 |       --   · θ⁻⁰(U⁻,U⁰) is the restriction of the reflected config to pos+int sites.\n1447 |       --\n1448 |       -- The operator T is POSITIVE because its integral kernel is a positive-definite\n1449 |       -- function on (SU(N)ⁿ × SU(N)ⁿ), which follows from the Peter–Weyl theorem\n1450 |       -- on SU(N) (OS 1979, Theorem 3.1).  Therefore ⟨g, T·g⟩ ≥ 0.\n1451 |       --\n1452 |       -- The proof of the identity uses:\n1453 |       --   1. `measure_factorization'` to change variables from μ₀ to μ⁺ × μ⁻ × μ⁰\n1454 |       --   2. The reflection lemmas `neg_action_reflection_os_periodic` and\n1455 |       --      `interface_action_reflection_symmetric_os_periodic`\n1456 |       --   3. The measure invariance under reflection (Haar measure is inversion-invariant)\n1457 |       --\n1458 |       -- FORMALIZATION STATUS:\n1459 |       --\n\n... 90 more line(s) below (use offset=1460 to continue reading)","timestamp":1784084518,"token_count":1186,"tool_call_id":"call_00_jbpX2N8dgaQ22OKto1dX5135","full_token_estim